# Writing a spec in this repository

These rules bind every spec the spec-writer authors here. `AGENTS.md` binds every
line the spec asks for; this file binds how the spec is written and which gates
each step runs.

## The preliminary spec is the spec's source

- The preliminary spec named by the prompt (`specs/<id>/.manual/NNN-*.md`) is
  binding and authoritative. Do not re-open anything it decides.
- Verify each fact you build on against the tree; do not assume it. Where the
  preliminary spec and the code disagree, the code is the baseline and the
  preliminary spec is the target.
- If something is genuinely ambiguous, pick the reading most consistent with the
  preliminary spec and `AGENTS.md`, and record it in the `.spec-md` problem
  statement. Do not ask.
- Its out-of-scope list is the operator's decision, not a deferral.

## Sections and steps

- You own the final plan. A section is at most about forty steps, and
  dependencies point backward.
- A section's paragraph in the preliminary spec is its spec. Put that
  paragraph's essentials into each brief, because mappers do not read each
  other's sections.
- Nothing is deferred (`AGENTS.md`). No brief hands part of its own work to a
  later step, and no step leaves a stub. Work that cannot be finished as written
  is a blocked step naming the question.
- Where the preliminary spec names a reuse, the brief says which code moves or
  is called, and what is deleted at the source in the same step, so the
  `reuse_critic` can check it.

## Section invariants

- Declare an invariant on every section AFTER the one that establishes its rule.
  It runs after every step, so never declare it on the section that makes it
  true.
- Commands contain no backslash; use `-F` for literal text.
- Every spec declares these on all its sections:
  - `ASSERT_INVARIANT elev-list-empty must-find-nothing: git grep -n -F "ELEV0" -- Elevation/elevation-known-findings.json`
  - `ASSERT_INVARIANT no-vstest must-find-nothing: git grep -n -E "Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk|xunit.runner.visualstudio|Xunit.SkippableFact|IsTestingPlatformApplication" -- "*.fsproj" "*.props" "*.targets"`
- Add one wherever a brief says "nothing may name X". The preliminary spec lists
  its own.

## Choosing a step's gates

A step runs only the gates that cover the seam of its change.

- **`build`** on every step that edits a file the solution compiles.
- **`elevation`** on every step that edits an `.fs` or `.fsproj`, and on no
  other step.
- **`elevation-full`** at the final step only. The scoped `elevation` already
  analyzes every project that references a changed one.
- **`constructor-unit-tests`** and **`ui-smoke`** (whole suites under a minute)
  on the steps that touch their code.
- **`ui-tests`** selected by the classes the step touches (`gate_selection`);
  whole at the final step only.
- **`unit-tests`** (`BerremanTests`, about 52 minutes whole; single inverse
  classes up to 997 s) selected by class only, on the steps its `applies_to`
  matches; whole at the final step only.
- **`kit-acceptance`** on the steps that touch the Study, run-law or import code,
  or the acceptance test files.
- **A step that edits no file runs no gate.** Its `how_to` never asks the worker
  to run tests or the elevation driver by hand, and its `acceptance` names only
  gates the step runs.

`count_at_least` baselines never regress. A test that moves between projects
counts in its new project in the same step.
